WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:00.800
Hello!

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.800
Ms. Coffee Bean and her team of archaeologists
discovered

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.640
an ancient Machine Learning paper
and the finding is interesting for 3 reasons:

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:16.400
Firstly, the paper solves a part of symbolic
mathematics with deep neural networks, surpassing

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.440
even the mighty Mathematica on the test set.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.800
Secondly, it is not really self-supervised
learning, but yet the amount of training data

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.240
for this task is virtually infinite, stick
around to see why and thirdly, it is one of

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.400
Ms. Coffee Bean’s favorite papers.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.360
I think she has a weak spot for mathematics,
hihi…

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.760
So, welcome to this AI Coffee Break

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.200
where you will find out&nbsp;
how artificial intelligence&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.080
tackles symbolic mathematics!

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:50.240
Deep learning can do symbolic mathematics,
or at least a part of it as far as this paper

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.680
goes, but why should anybody care about this?

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.200
Well, you should be hyped because of at least
two reasons:

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.600
First, mathematics follows rigorous rules
and is exact.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.000
Perhaps you remember that e to the power of
x integrated is again e to the power of x

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.080
plus a constant and nothing else would be
the answer here.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.360
But deep learning with its neural networks
is clearly statistical, which can be seen

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.280
as an opposite to rule-based systems, where
you could prove that you arrive at the right

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.840
answer 100% of the time.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:31.360
One more reason to care about neural networks
solving symbolic mathematics is exactly the

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.880
symbolic nature of this problem:
Neural networks had huge success on domains

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.240
where representations are continuous in the
first place, like in computer vision where

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.240
pixels take continuous values.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.040
Dark spots take low values, while bright pixels
have large values.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.520
But while neural networks perform&nbsp;
well on continous problems,&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.280
they are still struggling with symbolic reasoning,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.360
so with exactly the mathematical equations
this paper deals with, which are in forms

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.560
like x + y, which could
stand for any two numbers being added up.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.640
While the numerical expression is continuous,
as there are several other numbers between

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.960
2 and 3, x and y are the opposite of continuous,
they are symbols that can

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.680
stand for numbers
or even for other symbols, and there is nothing

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.680
in between the symbol x and the symbol y,
so to say.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.000
The only relationship defined here is stated
in the equation, but that’s about it.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.600
And neural networks so far, had been applied
on numeric tasks where the network gets as

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.360
input two numbers and the output is again
a number, like adding or multiplying two numbers,

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.840
and there they perform quite poorly.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.240
But this paper considers the
symbolic type of calculations where the input

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.240
is an equation describing the relationship
of symbols and the output is again in symbolic

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.280
form, but this time, representing the solution.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.480
And if you remember from mathematics classes,
depicted here is integration.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:18.160
Another symbolic mathematics problems are first
and second order ordinary differential equations

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.760
or short ODEs.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:25.120
Ms. Coffee Bean thinks that it is here, in
the problem choice where the genius of the

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.880
paper is condensed.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.320
For this video, we will stick with the integration
since we want to stay at a beginner level

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.600
and ODEs are renowned to scare people.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.800
For everyone interested in ODEs, check out
the paper linked in the video description.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.000
Disclaimer: it works very similar to integration.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.200
So, we think we agree that both integration
and ODEs are hard for humans and now we want

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.080
to solve symbolic integration with neural
networks.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.240
But neural networks require training data,
so pairs of functions and their integrals.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.920
Where to get that from?

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.200
We could generate some functions randomly
and take systems like MATLAB and Mathematica

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.960
to predict the integral.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:15.840
But these systems are not very fast and sometimes
fail even for integrable functions, so forward

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.160
generation is a good start for some training
data, but won’t get us far and we want to

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.240
be better than Mathematica.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.920
Therefore, a little out-of-the-box thinking
is required.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.600
Integration is hard, but the backward operation
to it, is differentiation.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.600
Which is fast, easy to compute, following
simple rules.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.320
So let’s use this lesson
and additionally to forward generation use

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:49.360
something else: by generating random functions
on the output side instead of the input side

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.680
and then computing their derivatives.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.960
These function pairs can be then added to
the training pool.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:02.160
This is cool and it’s called backward generation
and it can generate a lot of hard examples,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.280
but has the following problem:
It is very unlikely to randomly generate very

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:13.040
complicated examples in the first place and
even more unlikely is to have them simplify

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.840
to a very simple function in the derivative.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.880
It is important to have&nbsp;
these long-short combinations&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.680
too, otherwise the network could become biased

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.200
to long equations.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:30.160
To address this, the authors use another clever
trick: two random functions F and G are generated,

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.720
their derivatives small f and small g
are computed and these pairs

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.400
are added to the training set.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.720
But now, with the rule of integration by parts,
one can also add to the training set combinations

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:49.120
of these f and g functions like this: F times
G is already known because it is already in

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.840
the training set, only that the second term
is unknown.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:00.240
We know small f and the integral of g, but
we do not know the integral of f times G,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.560
BUT with enough luck it might already be in
the training set if this already contains

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.040
a lot of stuff generated with forward and
backward generation beforehand!

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.720
So every time the authors discover something
like this in the training set, they generate

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:21.120
a new data point by backward generation with
integration by parts.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.640
But Ms. Coffee Bean, wait a little: The authors
have now three means of generating as much

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.400
training data as they want with forward and
backward generation,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.480
also using integration by parts.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.560
But all this relies on randomly generating
functions which are then either integrated

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:39.840
or differentiated.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.160
So three questions remain:
How to generate functions randomly,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.280
and how to feed them into a neural network?

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.320
And what is this mysterious neural network
anyway?

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.280
Okay, we will answer
these questions one at a time.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:59.040
To randomly generate functions, we first need
a way to represent mathematical expressions.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:05.120
The choice here falls to trees where operators
and functions are internal nodes, the operands

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.880
are children and constants and variables are
leaves.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.840
The choice falls to trees because of lots
of reasons: Trees represent the order in which

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.840
operations are executed.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.000
Associativity is clear and parentheses become
obsolete.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:22.880
And because of this, they are very easy to
grow.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:28.480
On the other hand, expanding expressions in
the sequential representation, is very

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:34.080
clear at the both ends, but adding something
in between might get a little messy.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.720
So yeah, if one wants to grow an expression
randomly, one just has to randomly select

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.800
functions, operators and operands randomly
and just check that unary operands, like

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.000
sinuses or exponentials get only one child,&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.760
while binary operands like&nbsp;
addition or multiplication

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.120
get two children.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.240
Then on to the second question: How to process
mathematical expressions, now in form of trees,

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.760
by a neural network?

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.680
Well, we don’t process the trees.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:04.880
Wait, what?

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:12.000
The authors linearize the trees to a sequence
again by using prefix notation where each

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.640
node is written before its children, from
left to right, like in this example.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:22.080
The advantage over the common way of writing
mathematical expressions is that the prefix

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.440
notation does not require any parentheses
which would make the life of the neural network

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:32.080
harder by having to remember to close them
after they have been opened.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:33.120
So no trees?

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:40.080
Neural networks working on trees where very
en vogue in Natural Language Processing (NLP),

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:40.960
see Tree-LSTMs.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:47.040
But now, NLP is dominated by Transformers
and guess what: The neural network employed

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.440
to solve symbolic integration and ODEs is
a Transformer!

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.480
Haha, you did not expect that to happen, right?

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.560
Transformers are now everywhere.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.240
So yeah, the authors use&nbsp;
a transformer architecture&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.640
to translate from the input sequence to the

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:03.600
output sequence.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.680
And with the awesome data generation procedure
that can generate virtually infinite amounts

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:14.240
of it, there is enough data for the transformer
to learn the problem.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.040
It does not really matter&nbsp;
that symbolic integration&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.080
is hard (I think we all agree here), because

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:26.400
there is enough data, which proves to be
in this case 20 million examples.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:31.040
Yeah, they generated 20 million data samples,
just like that.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.280
This is why Ms. Coffee Bean
thinks that this is genius.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:39.040
Take this equation: You want to maximize research
results.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.280
You know transformers can do anything, so
of course you use them.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:50.080
You have your problem but you realize its
limited data is too little for the transformer

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:51.920
to learn something meaningful.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.960
But you still want to do good research.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.720
So you choose an application where you have
virtually infinite amounts of data and the

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.080
recipe to maximizing success is set!

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.520
Well, don't take Ms. Coffee Bean so seriously,
but it seems like this is kind of the recipe

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:10.960
for success nowadays.

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:13.920
And this is what partially&nbsp;
happens in self-supervised&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.680
learning because there you have almost infinite

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.760
amounts of training data.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:26.800
So yeah, in any case, the 20 million samples
that the authors here have, seem to be very

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.240
effective, because their transformer network
is able to solve the integration examples

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:40.000
with around 99% accuracy in less than a second
per example, while Mathematica (given 30

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.400
seconds of compute time) can only solve 84
percent, Maple 67 percent and

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.840
Matlab only 65 percent.

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:54.880
But where are the remaining 1 percent
in integration performance of the Transformer?

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.120
Well, we remember that we are in the statistical
world of neural networks.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:06.640
So, until we meet you next time, watch out
for outliers if you wander

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.640
out alone in this world…!

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.840
And hey, do not forget to like and subscribe!

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:28.297
Okay bye!

